(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "typescript-runtime");
  const prose = [
  "TypeScript adds checking and syntax to the development process, but the resulting program uses JavaScript's runtime behavior. A number is still a JavaScript number, and an object still follows JavaScript's rules. The type checker can reject suspicious source code before execution, yet it does not create a separate runtime that enforces every annotation. Understanding JavaScript is therefore essential: TypeScript helps describe and check the program, while JavaScript semantics determine what happens when the emitted code runs.",
  "An annotation such as count: number tells the checker what values are expected in the source program. The annotation is removed when the code is emitted as JavaScript, so it does not become a runtime check around every assignment. This explains why an external caller or untrusted response can still supply an unexpected value. If the running application must reject such a value, it needs executable validation in addition to the compile-time description.",
  "The compiler examines the code and types available during checking. It cannot inspect the future contents of a server response that arrives after the application starts. If that response claims an age is \"unknown\" instead of a number, an annotation does not repair or reject it automatically. Validate the received value at the boundary before relying on its shape. Static checking can then help the rest of the program use the validated result consistently.",
  "Parsing JSON establishes that the text has valid JSON syntax, not that it represents the object your application expects. The result might be null, an array, or an object missing required fields. Check the relevant fields and their types before treating it as trusted application data. A schema validator or carefully written guard can do that work. Simply asserting the desired TypeScript type changes the compiler's view without examining or transforming the actual value.",
  "If a function requires a number and source code passes a string, TypeScript can report that mismatch before the call executes. This shortens the feedback loop and documents the expected interface. The protection depends on the information supplied to the checker and on the build actually treating errors as failures. Values hidden behind any or unchecked assertions can bypass useful checking. TypeScript catches many mistakes, but a successful check is not proof that every runtime input is valid.",
  "An interface describes the members an object is expected to have. Declaring interface User with a name field does not allocate a user, initialize a field, or create a constructor called User. You still create actual values with JavaScript expressions or classes. The interface helps check those values in source code and then disappears from the emitted program. That separation explains why a type declaration cannot by itself provide runtime validation or object construction.",
  "The browser receives JavaScript after the build removes ordinary TypeScript annotations. It therefore has no annotation such as : number to consult when a value arrives. Runtime enforcement must be represented by actual JavaScript checks, such as verifying a type or range and rejecting invalid input. This is why browser security and data validation cannot depend on TypeScript declarations alone. The declarations guide development; executable code handles conditions encountered by the running application.",
  "A parsed JSON value may look plausible while still violating the expected shape. For example, a record can contain name as a number or omit id entirely. Before calling string methods or using the identifier, verify the fields your operation needs. Start from an unknown value and narrow it with real checks or a schema parser. Checking only that parsing succeeded leaves the application exposed to structurally valid JSON that is semantically wrong for its use.",
  "Writing a response type tells TypeScript what the developer expects the server to return. It does not cause the network layer to inspect every field against that declaration. A server bug or changed API can therefore produce a value that disagrees with the type. Treat the boundary as untrusted and validate the response when correctness requires that guarantee. After validation, the type becomes a useful description backed by evidence rather than an unchecked promise.",
  "A type assertion such as value as User asks the checker to treat the expression as a User. It does not add missing properties, convert field values, or create a User instance. If value is malformed, the malformed value remains. Assertions can be appropriate when the developer has information the checker cannot express, but they are not a substitute for checking external data. Use conversion or validation code when the runtime value actually needs to change or be verified.",
  "JavaScript's typeof operator executes and returns a description such as \"string\" or \"number\" for the current value. TypeScript can use that runtime test to narrow what is known within a branch. A type annotation, by contrast, is removed from emitted code and performs no test. The distinction matters when handling unknown input. Remember that typeof has JavaScript-specific behavior, such as reporting \"object\" for null, so some cases need additional checks.",
  "Array.isArray checks the actual runtime value and distinguishes arrays from ordinary objects. After that check, you know the container is an array, but you do not yet know the type or validity of every element. An array of strings still needs element checks before being treated as an array of trusted records. Build validation in layers: first establish the container, then establish the properties needed for the operations you plan to perform.",
  "A compiler diagnostic can reveal a bad call while the developer is editing or building, before a user encounters it. For example, passing a boolean to a function expecting a string is a mismatch the checker can identify from the source. Whether erroneous code is emitted depends on the toolchain and configuration, so the project should make type checking part of its required checks. The early warning is valuable only when the workflow pays attention to it.",
  "unknown lets the program hold a value without pretending to know its usable shape. Before calling a string method or reading a required property, the code must narrow or otherwise justify that use. A typeof check can establish that an unknown value is a string. This encourages explicit handling at uncertain boundaries. An assertion can still bypass the need for evidence, so using unknown is a helpful discipline rather than an automatic runtime validator.",
  "any tells the checker to allow operations it would otherwise question. If a network result is any, code can call a nonexistent method without receiving a useful type error. The method can still fail at runtime because any changes checking, not JavaScript behavior. Use it deliberately when necessary and keep the unchecked area limited. For genuinely uncertain input, unknown usually makes the need for validation or narrowing more visible to the rest of the program.",
  "A parameter annotation describes the calls the checker should accept. If total expects an array of numbers, passing a known array of strings can be rejected during development. The annotation does not become an automatic runtime barrier, and JavaScript callers or unchecked values can still violate it. When a function sits at an untrusted boundary, validate the input there. Within checked code, the parameter type helps callers and implementers agree on the expected contract.",
  "instanceof tests a value against a runtime constructor and its prototype relationship. An interface supplies no constructor because it is only a compile-time description and is erased. You therefore cannot use instanceof User when User is only an interface. Check the relevant properties or use another appropriate runtime discriminator instead. A class is different because it can have a constructor at runtime, although a class check still answers a different question from structural validation of arbitrary JSON.",
  "A useful guard performs a test that justifies treating a value more specifically. Checking typeof value === \"string\" supports using string operations within that branch. For object data, the guard must check the properties its claimed type requires. TypeScript can also trust a user-written type predicate whose implementation is wrong, so the author's responsibility remains important. Narrowing is valuable when the checkerâ€™s conclusion matches the runtime evidence actually collected.",
  "The parameter is unknown, so the function cannot safely assume it supports toUpperCase. The typeof test checks the current value, and only the string branch calls the method. Other values fall through without that call. This connects runtime validation with static narrowing: the emitted JavaScript performs the check, while TypeScript uses it to permit the operation in the guarded branch. The annotation alone would not make calling the method safe.",
  "Compilation checks the program under its declared assumptions, including any types assigned to external responses. It does not contact every future server response and prove those assumptions true. An API can return a missing field or a value of the wrong type after compilation has succeeded. Validate the response where it enters the application, then use the checked representation internally. Static checking and runtime validation support each other because they address different sources of uncertainty."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/typescript-runtime");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "typescript-structure");
  const prose = [
  "TypeScript usually decides whether an object fits a type by checking its required members and their types. An object with numeric x and y fields can fit a point interface even if its declaration never names that interface. This is structural typing. It differs from requiring a matching class name or explicit inheritance relationship. The checker examines compatibility of the shape, while some features, such as private class members, introduce additional restrictions.",
  "One object can satisfy several interfaces when it has the members each requires. An object with numeric x and y plus a string name can be used where either a point-like or named value is expected. It does not need to declare membership in both interfaces. Each use asks whether the required shape is present. This lets functions depend on the information they need without forcing unrelated objects into a shared inheritance hierarchy.",
  "A union such as string | number allows a value from either alternative. That does not mean every operation supported by one alternative is safe on the whole union. Calling toUpperCase would fail when the value is a number. Check which case you have before using case-specific behavior. Thinking of a union as several possible sets of values helps explain both its flexibility at assignment and the need for narrowing before certain operations.",
  "A runtime check can distinguish alternatives in a union. If value is string | number, a typeof value === \"string\" branch establishes the string case, allowing string methods there. The other branch can handle numbers. TypeScript follows the control flow and narrows its view based on the check. The value itself is not converted by narrowing; the program has learned which kind it already has at that point.",
  "Two classes can be compatible when their instances expose the same required public members, even without one extending the other. A function needing a public read method can often accept either instance. This follows TypeScript's structural approach. Private and protected members can impose additional compatibility rules, so public resemblance is not the entire story for every class. Also distinguish instance compatibility from equality of constructors or runtime instanceof behavior.",
  "An interface helps the checker reason about object structure, but the emitted JavaScript contains no interface object to inspect. Runtime code cannot ask whether a value was declared as that interface. It can inspect actual properties, use a discriminator, or test a real class constructor where appropriate. This matters especially for external data: a compile-time interface does not become metadata that automatically verifies an incoming object's fields.",
  "Pointlike requires numeric x and y members. The point object provides both, so its additional label does not prevent it from being used through that interface in the shown variable-based assignment. A function accepting Pointlike can rely on x and y without needing label. TypeScript also applies special excess-property checks to fresh object literals in certain contexts, so distinguish ordinary structural compatibility from those checks when interpreting a diagnostic.",
  "The name assigned to an interface is not usually the basis for object compatibility. A value fits when its members satisfy the required shape. For example, a position object with numeric x and y can serve a function expecting a point even if it came from an unrelated module. This encourages small interfaces that describe actual needs. It also means naming two structurally identical types differently does not automatically make their values incompatible.",
  "A function needing only an id can often accept an existing object that also contains a name and timestamp. The extra fields do not remove the required id. Inside the function, the smaller interface limits what the checker lets it assume about the value. Fresh object literals may receive additional excess-property checks intended to catch mistakes, so the exact assignment context matters. Extra data is not universally forbidden, but neither is every literal accepted without scrutiny.",
  "If an interface requires both x and y, an object containing only x cannot satisfy it. The missing member matters even if every existing field has the correct type. The function receiving the object is entitled to use y, so allowing the assignment would undermine its contract. Add the required value, change the interface if absence is legitimate, or use a different type. An assertion can silence the complaint but cannot create the missing property.",
  "Marking a property optional means an object may omit it. A user record with an optional nickname can therefore be valid without one. Code that uses the nickname must account for that absence, for example by choosing a fallback or checking before calling string methods. Optional does not mean a value is always present but merely unimportant. Compiler settings can affect assignment details involving undefined, so the core guarantee is that callers cannot assume the member exists.",
  "A union value may belong to any of its alternatives. If one variant has a message field and another has a count field, reading message without a check is unsafe. Narrow the union using a discriminant or another valid test before accessing variant-specific members. This requirement follows from the possible runtime values, not from arbitrary compiler strictness. Operations common to all alternatives can generally be used without choosing a particular case first.",
  "A discriminant is a field whose distinct literal values identify union variants. A result might be { kind: \"success\", data: ... } or { kind: \"failure\", error: ... }. Checking kind lets TypeScript associate the branch with the appropriate remaining fields. The discriminator must accurately represent the value at runtime, especially for external data. Once validated, this pattern makes each case explicit and helps prevent reading fields that do not exist in another variant.",
  "An intersection A & B requires a value to satisfy both types at once. Combining a named type with a positioned type requires both the name and coordinate members. This differs from a union, which permits either alternative. If the requirements conflict, the intersection can become impossible to satisfy; combining a property that must be both an incompatible string and number does not magically convert it. Read the combined obligations carefully rather than treating intersection as simple text merging.",
  "A public method is part of what other code can do with an instance, so it contributes to the instance's structural type. A function expecting an object with save(): void can check whether that operation is available with a compatible signature. The class name alone is not enough if the method is missing or incompatible. Conversely, an unrelated object may fit by providing the required method. Runtime implementation correctness is still a separate concern from signature compatibility.",
  "Private members carry information about their declaration origin when TypeScript checks class compatibility. Two classes can expose identical public methods yet remain incompatible because their private members come from different declarations. This prevents some assignments that ordinary structural comparison of public fields would permit. It does not mean all TypeScript classes are nominally typed. The language combines its usual structural rules with additional restrictions for features whose visibility and origin matter.",
  "A fresh object literal supplied where a particular type is expected receives an extra check for unexpected properties. If an options type expects color but the literal supplies colour, that check can catch a likely spelling mistake. Ordinary structural compatibility of an existing variable can allow additional members in other contexts. This distinction explains why moving a literal into a variable can change the diagnostic, though fixing the underlying contract is better than using that behavior to hide a real mistake.",
  "The typeof expression is an actual JavaScript test, so it can establish which primitive alternative a union contains at runtime. TypeScript uses the branch condition to narrow the value within that branch. For string | number, testing for string permits string operations there without changing the value. Be aware of the operator's limits: arrays and null need more specific handling than simply checking for \"object\" when validating a detailed structure.",
  "The in operator checks whether a named property exists on an object, including its prototype chain. TypeScript can use that test to narrow a union when the property distinguishes relevant variants. It does not automatically validate the property's value or every other required field. Optional properties can also appear on more than one side of a narrowing. Use the check for the evidence it actually supplies, and add further validation when the runtime data is untrusted.",
  "A function should often accept the smallest shape it genuinely needs. If it only reads a record's id, an interface requiring id allows callers to pass larger records without the function depending on all their details. That makes the function reusable and limits assumptions. The additional fields remain on the runtime object; the smaller type does not strip them away. Fresh-literal excess-property rules can still affect particular calls, so structural compatibility and literal checking must be distinguished."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/typescript-structure");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "react-components");
  const prose = [
  "A function component describes a piece of interface from its current inputs and state. For example, a Badge component can return a strong element containing a label. React uses that description to render the interface and can ask for another description when data changes. Components may also return other renderable values, including null when nothing should appear. The important idea is that the function describes UI; it should not manually rebuild the browser page on every call.",
  "In JSX, a capitalized name tells the transform to refer to a JavaScript component, while a lowercase name is treated as a built-in tag name. Writing Badge therefore refers to your Badge function; writing badge does not invoke that function in the same way. This convention is part of how JSX distinguishes custom components from browser elements. Capitalizing the function alone is not enough if the JSX tag uses a different or unresolved name.",
  "JSX lets a JavaScript file describe element structure with markup-like syntax. A component can combine that structure with JavaScript expressions to show data, choose branches, or produce a list. The browser does not ordinarily execute raw JSX; the build transforms it into JavaScript that creates React element descriptions. JSX is therefore a convenient source syntax, not a separate HTML document. Its rules differ from HTML in places because it participates in JavaScript expressions.",
  "A return expression containing two adjacent JSX tags is ambiguous because JavaScript expects one expression there. Wrap the siblings in a parent element or a fragment so the expression describes one grouped result. A div adds a real DOM element, while a fragment groups the children without that extra node. Choose according to the intended structure and layout. React can also render arrays, but simply placing two tags side by side is not an array expression.",
  "React DOM uses className to assign CSS classes in JSX. For example, className=\"warning\" applies the warning class to the resulting element. The difference is syntax at the component description level; the browser still receives an ordinary element with CSS classes. When the class depends on data, pass an expression such as className={isLate ? \"late\" : \"ready\"}. Remembering the prop name avoids confusing JSX attributes with literal HTML pasted unchanged into JavaScript.",
  "A component can compose a larger interface by including another component in its returned JSX. A page might render a Header, a List, and a Footer, each responsible for its own portion. This keeps the page's structure readable and lets those pieces be reused. The child can receive data through props rather than reaching into the parentâ€™s local variables. Composition works best when the boundaries describe useful responsibilities rather than split markup into arbitrary fragments.",
  "Writing <Badge /> tells React to use the Badge component identified by that JavaScript name. A lowercase <badge /> is treated as a tag name instead, so it does not mean the same thing. Import or define the component and use the matching capitalized identifier in JSX. This keeps custom component references distinct from built-in elements such as span. The syntax creates a description for React to render rather than calling the function as an ordinary helper directly.",
  "Rendering should calculate the interface from inputs without changing unrelated state. If a component increments a global counter every time it renders, asking React to render again can alter behavior even when its inputs have not changed. That makes output harder to predict and can expose problems when renders are repeated. Keep side effects in appropriate event handlers or synchronization mechanisms. A pure render can be evaluated again without changing the world merely by describing the UI.",
  "Curly braces in JSX switch from markup-like text to a JavaScript expression. Writing <p>{student.name}</p> displays the value of student.name rather than the literal characters \"student.name\". The expression can calculate a value or call a pure helper, but a statement such as an if declaration does not fit directly in the same slot. Compute it beforehand or use an expression such as a conditional. This lets presentation follow ordinary JavaScript data and logic.",
  "A fragment groups several children in JSX without creating an additional browser element. That is useful when a component should return a heading and paragraph but an extra div would interfere with the desired DOM structure or styling. The fragment is a grouping device for React's description. When a fragment itself represents a keyed list item, use the explicit Fragment form that can receive a key; the shorthand empty tags do not accept that attribute.",
  "A component's returned elements describe what should appear, including nested components and built-in tags. React evaluates the relevant components and compares the resulting description with previous work before updating the DOM as needed. The returned tree is therefore a description, not a collection of manually created DOM nodes. Understanding that separation helps explain why rendering can run again without requiring every visible element to be destroyed and recreated.",
  "Defining a component function inside another component creates a new function object whenever the outer component renders. React can then see a different component type in the same position and reset the inner component's state. Define reusable component functions at module scope and pass needed data as props. Nesting their JSX is fine; the problem is repeatedly defining the component type inside a render. That distinction avoids confusing visual nesting with unstable component identity.",
  "A lowercase JSX tag such as section describes a built-in element name. React DOM uses it to create or update the corresponding browser element. A capitalized identifier such as Section refers to a JavaScript component that must be in scope. These are different instructions even when the names look similar. Use lowercase for the platform element and uppercase for your component so the transform can tell which meaning you intend.",
  "Returning null lets a component participate in the tree without producing visible output for that render. A warning component might return null when there is no warning to show and return a message otherwise. This is often clearer than returning an empty wrapper solely to satisfy syntax. The component still runs when rendered, so null does not mean its function was never evaluated. It means the render result contributes no visible element at that point.",
  "A component can choose its output using ordinary JavaScript conditions. If a user is signed in, it might return account controls; otherwise it might return a sign-in button. The selected branch describes the UI for the current render. When the relevant data changes, another render can choose differently. Keep the condition tied to actual inputs or state rather than manually hiding stale markup after rendering, which creates another representation to keep synchronized.",
  "JSX braces can supply a computed attribute value, such as src={avatarUrl} or src={buildAvatarUrl(user.id)}. The expression is evaluated during rendering, and its result becomes the prop value. Quoting the expression instead would produce literal text. Make sure the calculation is appropriate for rendering and that required data is available. The same mechanism works for numbers, booleans, objects, and functions where the receiving prop expects those values.",
  "A child component does not automatically receive every local variable from the component that renders it. The parent passes the data the child needs as props, such as <Badge label={status} />. This makes the child's input contract visible and allows it to work in another parent with different data. Avoid relying on accidental shared variables when a prop describes the dependency clearly. The child can then focus on how to display or use the supplied value.",
  "One component function can describe many similar pieces of UI by receiving different props. A Profile component can display different people without duplicating its markup for each one. Each rendered instance receives its own inputs, and local state, if any, belongs to that instance. Reuse does not require every instance to look identical. It means the common behavior is expressed once while the varying information is supplied explicitly.",
  "Badge is a capitalized identifier, so <Badge /> refers to the custom function defined above Page. Badge returns a strong element containing New, which becomes part of Page's rendered output. If the tag were lowercase, JSX would treat it as an element name rather than that function reference. This small example shows composition: Page describes where the badge belongs, while Badge owns the markup that represents it.",
  "When a component returns a heading immediately followed by a paragraph, those siblings need to be represented by one valid JavaScript expression. A fragment can group them without adding a layout wrapper, or a real parent element can provide needed semantics and styling. The issue is the syntax of adjacent JSX expressions, not a rule that every component must add a visible container. React can render other grouped forms, such as an array of keyed elements, when written explicitly."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/react-components");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "react-data");
  const prose = [
  "Props are the inputs a parent supplies when rendering a child. A task list can pass a task title and completion status to each task row. The row then describes its UI using those values without needing to know where they were loaded. This creates a clear direction for data flow and a visible contract between components. If the parent later supplies different values, the child can render the updated information from those new inputs.",
  "A child should treat received props as a snapshot it does not own. If it mutates an object passed by the parent, it can alter data other components also use without the parent making an explicit update. That creates confusing and potentially stale UI. To request a change, the child can call a provided callback. The owner then updates state and passes new props, keeping responsibility for the data clear.",
  "Inside JSX, braces mean \"evaluate this JavaScript expression.\" For example, {task.title} uses the current title, while text outside braces is displayed literally. This allows calculations and conditions to sit alongside markup without maintaining a separate template language. The slot expects an expression, not arbitrary statements. If the logic is complex, compute a clear value before returning JSX so the rendered structure remains easy to read.",
  "A key tells React which rendered list item corresponds to which underlying item across updates. If tasks move, a stable task identifier lets React associate each row with the same task rather than its old position. That matters for local state and DOM behavior such as an edited input. Keys must be unique among the relevant siblings and stable for the same item. Generating a fresh random key during every render defeats that identity.",
  "Filtering selects which data items should be represented before mapping them to elements. If the user wants only unfinished tasks, filter the task array using the completion flag, then render the remaining items. This makes the visible list a direct result of current data and criteria. The filter should not mutate the original state. When either the data or filter changes, rendering derives the appropriate list again without maintaining another independent copy of the same selection.",
  "An item's position can change when another item is inserted, removed, or sorted. If position is used as the key, React may associate an existing row's state with a different item after that change. A stable identifier follows the item instead of the slot. For example, task 42 keeps key 42 whether it appears first or fifth. Index keys can be acceptable for genuinely static lists, but they are risky when item identity must survive reordering.",
  "Each li receives task.id as its key, so React can match that task with its previous rendered row when the array changes. The displayed name can change without changing identity, and moving the task does not require treating it as a different item. The identifier must be stable and unique among these siblings. The key is used by React for reconciliation; if a child component also needs the identifier as data, pass it separately as an ordinary prop.",
  "The parent owns the value it passes, while the child receives it for rendering. A child cannot update the parent's state by assigning a different value to its prop variable, and mutating a shared object is an unsafe substitute. Instead, the child can report an action through a callback, such as onRename(newName). The parent applies the update and renders with new props. This keeps the displayed value and the authoritative data under one explicit owner.",
  "A list key should describe the item rather than where it currently sits. Suppose task A moves below task B: A should keep its key so React can preserve A's associated state. If its key changes with the index, React may preserve state for the position instead. This is especially visible with editable rows. Stable keys make reordering understandable to React, while changing a key deliberately tells it that the identity should be replaced.",
  "A database identifier often works well as a key because it already distinguishes one record from another and remains the same when its fields change. A student's display name is weaker because names can change or repeat. Use the identifier consistently for the same record among siblings. It is not enough for a value to be unique once; a key that is regenerated on each render causes React to treat the row as a new item repeatedly.",
  "The element returned directly from map represents one item in the list, so that is where React needs the key. If map returns a div containing a Task component, putting the key only on Task does not identify the outer div among its siblings. Move the key to the div or return the keyed component directly. For multiple siblings per item, an explicit keyed Fragment can provide the group without adding an unnecessary DOM wrapper.",
  "filter chooses data and map turns each chosen item into an element. For example, tasks.filter(isOverdue).map(renderTask) describes a list containing only overdue tasks. These are ordinary JavaScript array operations used to derive the current UI. Keep the callbacks appropriate for rendering and give each returned item a stable key. The result should follow from the current inputs, rather than update a second stored list that can fall out of sync with the original tasks.",
  "A component can share one layout while displaying different content from props. A TaskRow might render one task's title and due date in one place and another task's data elsewhere. The function remains reusable because it does not hard-code a particular record. Define the input contract around the information it actually needs. If variations require unrelated behavior and many special cases, reconsider whether one component still represents a coherent responsibility.",
  "JSX placed between a component's opening and closing tags arrives through its children prop. A Panel can render a title and then {children}, allowing callers to supply different content inside the same frame. The panel does not need to know every possible child component in advance. This is useful for composition and layout. children is still input from the parent, so the same expectations about treating props as read-only apply.",
  "The props available during a render describe the inputs for that particular render. An event handler created there also closes over values from that execution. Later renders can receive new props, but they do not rewrite local variables inside an earlier execution. Thinking in snapshots helps explain why directly mutating a prop is unsafe and why delayed callbacks can observe older values. The owner supplies updated data through a new render rather than changing the meaning of the old one.",
  "Suppose an editable list uses indices as keys and a new item is inserted at the beginning. The row formerly at index 0 now represents a different item, but React may preserve the old row's local state at that position. The user's draft text can appear attached to the wrong record. A stable record identifier avoids that mismatch by keeping identity tied to the data. This is why index keys are dangerous for lists whose membership or order changes.",
  "Choose a key from a property that identifies the underlying item across renders. An existing record id is usually better than its label, current position, or a random value generated while rendering. Labels can duplicate, positions can move, and fresh random values constantly replace identity. The key only needs to be unique in the relevant sibling list. Its purpose is to let React connect the new description with the same item's previous state and DOM work.",
  "A function prop lets a parent give a child a way to report an action without handing over ownership of the data. A row can call onDelete(task.id), and the parent decides how to update its task list. Passing the function itself is different from calling it during rendering. This arrangement keeps the child's interface reusable: it reports intent through the callback, while the parent supplies the behavior appropriate to its application.",
  "map can turn an array of records into an array of React elements, and JSX can include that array in the rendered result. Each element should have a key appropriate to its underlying item. For example, mapping tasks to rows expresses the same structure without manually writing a row for every task. The callback still needs to return its element; using braces without an explicit return is a common reason a mapped list unexpectedly renders nothing.",
  "The visible list should normally be calculated from the current records and selection criteria during rendering. If a completed-only view is stored separately from the task state, every task update must also synchronize that extra list. Deriving it avoids that extra responsibility and the bugs caused by forgetting an update. Store the information that changes independently, such as the selected filter, and calculate values that follow from it. Expensive calculations can be optimized without creating competing sources of truth."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/react-data");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "react-state");
  const prose = [
  "An event prop should receive a function React can call when the event occurs. Writing onClick={handleClick} supplies that function; writing onClick={handleClick()} calls it immediately during rendering and supplies its return value instead. The distinction explains why a button can trigger work before anyone clicks it. If you need to pass arguments, provide a wrapper such as () => handleClick(id), which is still a function to run later.",
  "A local variable is recreated when a component function runs again, so it cannot by itself preserve interactive information across renders. State gives React a place to retain that information for the component's identity in the tree. A counter can therefore remember its value after a click schedules another render. Different instances normally have separate state. Moving, replacing, or changing the identity of the component can reset that memory, so state is not simply attached to the function name globally.",
  "Calling a setter tells React what state to use for subsequent rendering. It does not rewrite the current render's local state variable in place. If count is 2 in an event handler, calling setCount(3) still leaves that handler's captured count as 2. A later render receives the updated state. This snapshot model explains why code immediately reading the old variable after a setter may appear unchanged even though the UI will update.",
  "If an array already held in state is changed with push, the same array object remains. Passing that reference back to the setter may let React treat the state as unchanged, and the mutation also alters data associated with earlier render snapshots. Create a new array that contains the intended items instead. This makes the update explicit and preserves the previous value. Immutability is about the state objects being shared across renders, not a ban on every local temporary mutation.",
  "When the next value depends on the state already queued, pass an updater function. setCount(previous => previous + 1) asks React to apply the increment to that queued value rather than to a count captured by an earlier render. This is especially useful for several updates in one event. The updater should be pure because React may evaluate it more than once in development checks. It should calculate the next state without performing unrelated side effects.",
  "If a search box and results panel both need the current query, giving each its own copy creates synchronization work. Put the query in their closest common parent and pass it to both. The search box reports edits through a callback, and the parent updates the shared state. Both children then receive the same next value. Lift only the state that needs coordination; unrelated local details can remain in the components that use them.",
  "The updater receives the state value React has reached after processing earlier queued updates. It returns that value plus one, so several queued increments build on each other. By contrast, setCount(count + 1) calculates from the count captured by the current render, which can produce the same replacement value repeatedly. The updater form expresses a transformation rather than a fixed replacement. Keep that transformation free of side effects so it remains safe for React to evaluate.",
  "Rendering happens when React asks a component to describe its UI, not when the user necessarily clicks anything. Calling handleClick() while assigning an event prop therefore runs the handler too early. It can trigger state updates during rendering or pass an unsuitable return value as the handler. Supply the function itself, or a wrapper that calls it later with arguments. Then the action occurs in response to the actual event.",
  "Each render sees a snapshot of state. A setter queues an update for React to process, so the current function execution continues with the values it already received. For example, logging count immediately after setCount(count + 1) can print the previous count. That does not mean the setter failed. Reason about the next render separately, and use an updater when calculating a new value from queued state rather than assuming the setter mutates the current variable.",
  "Suppose count is 0 and one handler calls setCount(count + 1) twice. Both expressions calculate 1 from the same captured 0, so the queue contains two requests to replace the value with 1. The result is not necessarily 2. Two updater calls, each returning previous + 1, express two sequential increments instead. This difference follows from state snapshots and queue processing, not from JavaScript forgetting how addition works.",
  "An updater function is applied to the state produced by earlier updates in the queue. If the queued value is 4, previous => previous + 1 returns 5, which becomes the input to a later updater. This lets several transformations compose correctly without relying on a stale captured variable. The function should depend on its argument and necessary stable inputs, and it should not perform actions such as sending a request merely because React evaluates the update.",
  "Replacing a state array means creating a new container for the intended next contents. To append a task, construct [...tasks, newTask] instead of pushing into tasks. React receives a different reference, and older renders still refer to their unchanged array. Nested objects also need care: copying the array alone does not make its existing elements new objects. Copy any element whose fields you change so the update preserves the relevant previous structure too.",
  "filter returns a new array containing items that pass its condition. To remove task 7, keep the tasks whose id is not 7 and pass that new array to the setter. The old state array remains unchanged, which makes the update consistent with React's snapshot model. Check that the condition uses the correct identity and has no side effects. Removing an item from the displayed array is distinct from deleting it from persistent storage, which may require separate application logic.",
  "map lets you construct a new array while replacing only the item that needs an edit. For the matching id, return a new object with the changed field; for other items, return the existing object. This preserves unchanged identities and avoids mutating an object shared with older renders. Merely mapping to the same objects and then changing one object's property would still mutate previous state. The new container and the changed element both matter.",
  "React can compare a proposed state value with the previous one by identity. If you change a property on the existing object and pass that same object back, the reference has not changed, so the update may be skipped. The mutation also makes prior snapshots unreliable. Create a new object with the intended fields instead. This gives React a distinct next value and keeps the previous state intact for code that still refers to it.",
  "A click handler is a function registered for the click event. React calls it when that event reaches the relevant element, allowing it to respond by updating state or performing other appropriate work. Rendering the button should only supply the handler, not execute its action. This separation keeps the UI description predictable and the interaction tied to the user's event. A wrapper is useful when the handler needs information such as the identity of the clicked item.",
  "Rendering two Counter components normally creates two separate stateful instances. Updating one does not automatically update the other, even though both use the same function definition. React associates state with each component's identity and position in the rendered tree. If both counters should always show one value, move that value to a common owner and pass it down. Local state is useful for independence; shared state is needed when the behavior must be coordinated.",
  "If a total is completely determined by the current items, calculate it from those items during rendering. Storing the total separately means every addition, removal, and edit must also update it correctly. Forgetting one path creates disagreement between the list and the displayed total. Keeping only the underlying items as state avoids that duplicate responsibility. If calculation becomes expensive, optimize the derivation while retaining one authoritative set of data.",
  "The expression setOpen(!open) runs as JavaScript while the component is rendering. Its return value is then supplied to onClick, rather than a function waiting for a click. That can cause immediate updates and repeated rendering instead of the intended interaction. Use a handler such as () => setOpen(previous => !previous). The wrapper delays execution until the event, and the updater calculates the toggle from the state React supplies at update time.",
  "push changes the existing array and returns its new length, which is another reason it is unsuitable as a direct replacement expression for array state. Build a new array with the added item and pass that array to the setter. If the update depends on queued state, use an updater such as previous => [...previous, item]. This preserves older snapshots and ensures the next value is still an array containing the intended items."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/react-state");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "react-sharing");
  const prose = [
  "When siblings display or edit the same information, a common parent can own one authoritative value. For example, a filter control and a results list can both use the parent's selected filter. The control reports changes, and the parent passes the updated filter to both children. This avoids maintaining two copies that must be synchronized manually. The children can still keep unrelated local state, such as whether a help panel is open.",
  "After lifting state, the parent supplies its current value to each child through props. A temperature display and an editor can therefore render from the same temperature rather than independently guessing each other's state. Props carry the snapshot for the current render; the parent remains responsible for updates. This makes the direction of data flow clear and keeps children reusable, since another parent can supply the same props from a different source.",
  "A child can report an intended change without owning the shared state. It might call onSelect(courseId), a function supplied by its parent. The parent then decides whether and how to update its selection. This separates the child's interaction from the application's state ownership. Passing a callback is not direct mutation of a prop; it is a defined channel for requesting work from the component that has authority over the value.",
  "If the displayed subtotal follows entirely from items and their prices, calculate it from those inputs. Storing it separately requires every item change to keep the subtotal synchronized. The same principle applies to a filtered list or a formatted label. Deriving values during rendering reduces opportunities for contradictory state. Store information that changes independently, and compute information that follows from it; optimize expensive computations only when there is a demonstrated need.",
  "Suppose two siblings each initialize their own selectedCourse value. Changing the selection in one does not automatically change the other, so the interface can show conflicting answers. Synchronizing the copies with extra effects adds more update paths to manage. A single common owner avoids that duplication. Both siblings read the same value and request changes through the owner, making agreement a consequence of the design rather than a separate maintenance task.",
  "A state update asks React to calculate the interface for the new state and commit the necessary visible changes. The application should describe what the UI looks like for that value rather than manually patch unrelated DOM nodes to keep them in sync. Updates can be queued and batched, so the current handler still sees its render's snapshot. The resulting render gives affected components consistent inputs from which to describe the next interface.",
  "Imagine a task editor and a preview shown side by side. If their parent owns the task being edited, it can pass the same current task to both. An edit updates that one value, and both views receive the new snapshot. If each view owns an independent copy, the application must invent a synchronization rule. Shared ownership at the common parent makes the intended agreement explicit while preserving separate responsibilities for editing and displaying.",
  "A callback prop gives the child a way to send information about an interaction upward. For example, an input can call onTitleChange(event.target.value), and its parent updates the title state. The child does not need to know whether the parent also saves the value or updates a preview. The callback is the interface between those responsibilities. Use a clear name and argument contract so the request is understandable at both ends.",
  "The closest common ancestor of components needing the same value is often a good owner because it can supply that value to all of them without moving unrelated state unnecessarily high. If two panels share a selection, their enclosing page may own it. If only one field uses an error hint, that hint can remain local. Choose ownership according to who must coordinate, rather than putting all application state in one place by default.",
  "A subtotal can be computed by summing the current item quantities and prices. If the items change, the next render naturally computes a new subtotal. Storing another subtotal state variable creates a second value that can drift when one update path forgets to change it. The derived calculation keeps the relationship explicit. If rounding or discounts affect the rule, include them in the calculation rather than relying on an independently updated display number.",
  "Celsius and Fahrenheit represent the same temperature, so storing both independently allows inconsistent combinations. Store one authoritative temperature and derive the other using the conversion formula. If the user can edit either field, convert the edit into the chosen representation or store a value together with its scale. The key is preserving one coherent fact. Two independently updated numbers require extra synchronization and can disagree after partial or incorrectly ordered updates.",
  "A controlled input receives its displayed value from React data, commonly state owned by a parent. Its change handler reports what the user entered so that owner can supply a new value. The browser's temporary edit is therefore reconciled with the authoritative prop on rendering. If the handler never updates the value, the input can appear unable to accept edits. The controlling component must complete the loop from event to state update to new prop.",
  "An input's onChange handler can read the proposed text and call a callback supplied by its parent. The parent updates the shared value, which may also drive a preview or validation message. This keeps the input's role focused on reporting interaction while the parent owns the resulting state. For a controlled input, the parent must pass the updated value back. Reporting the event without updating the controlling value leaves the visible field tied to the old snapshot.",
  "Props carry values from the component that renders a child to that child. A page can pass a selected item to a detail panel, which can pass relevant fields to smaller components. This downward flow makes dependencies visible in the rendered tree. Changes are usually requested through callbacks rather than by children rewriting those props. Keeping the two directions distinct helps explain where a displayed value came from and which component is responsible for changing it.",
  "A callback prop exposes an operation the child can request, such as onRemove(id) or onToggle(). The parent implements that operation using its own state and rules. This lets the child remain useful in different contexts without directly controlling shared data. The callback should describe intent clearly enough that the parent does not need to infer what happened. Passing a function provides a communication path; it does not transfer ownership of the parent's state to the child.",
  "If a selected item is stored once, every view can derive its display from that same choice. If several components store separate selections, each update must find and synchronize all of them, creating opportunities for disagreement. One authoritative value makes consistency simpler. It does not require eliminating every local state variable: independent UI details still belong locally. The principle applies specifically to multiple representations of the same changing fact.",
  "Suppose a component receives firstName and lastName and displays their combination. It can calculate the full name directly from those props on each render. Copying the result into local state adds a synchronization problem when either prop changes. Derived values usually do not need their own state because the current inputs already determine them. Local state is appropriate when there is genuinely independent information, such as an unsaved edit that intentionally differs from the supplied value.",
  "A sibling's local state belongs to that rendered instance and is not automatically available to another sibling. Reaching into its implementation would create a fragile dependency. If both need the same value, move it to a common owner or use another explicit sharing mechanism appropriate to the application. Then each component receives the information it needs through a defined interface. This makes the relationship clear instead of relying on hidden knowledge of another component's internals.",
  "Panel creates open with useState, so Panel owns the shared value. It passes open to both Toggle and Preview, and gives Toggle setOpen as its change callback. When Toggle requests a change, Panel can render again and supply the same new value to both children. Preview does not need to inspect Toggle's internals. The example demonstrates that the owner is the component holding the state, even when the interaction that changes it occurs in a child.",
  "One parent state change can produce new props for several children during the resulting render. A selected course might update the heading, detail panel, and action buttons together because all are derived from that shared selection. The parent does not need to issue an independent state update to each child. React determines the resulting rendering work from the tree. This coordination is easier to reason about than separate child copies that are updated one after another and may disagree."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/react-sharing");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "frontend-development").sets.find(s => s.id === "typescript-ts-vs-tsx");
  const prose = [
  "The Badge function contains <span> markup inside TypeScript source. The .tsx extension tells TypeScript to parse that JSX syntax, while .ts uses the plain TypeScript grammar. The parameter annotation is ordinary TypeScript; the span expression is what makes TSX necessary here. The project also needs an appropriate JSX build configuration. Renaming a file identifies the syntax it contains, but does not by itself install React or configure every step of the build.",
  "The source contains a JSX element, but the .ts extension selects a grammar that does not parse that element as JSX. That mismatch causes a compile error before the browser is involved. Use .tsx for this TypeScript file or move the markup into a suitable module. The problem is unrelated to whether the badge variable is exported or whether span is a valid HTML element. It is about how the compiler reads the source syntax.",
  "Both extensions describe source files used by the development toolchain. Ordinary TypeScript type syntax is removed, and JSX must eventually be transformed before a normal browser can execute the result. Some configurations preserve JSX temporarily for a later build step, so the TypeScript compiler itself does not always finish that transformation. The important endpoint is browser-executable JavaScript. Choosing .tsx enables JSX parsing; it does not introduce a different runtime for the component.",
  "A Workout class can contain fields, a constructor, and methods without containing any JSX. Those are ordinary TypeScript and JavaScript concepts, so a .ts file is a natural home for the model. A component can import the model from a .tsx file without requiring the model itself to use that extension. Keeping the model independent of presentation also makes it easier to use in tests or other non-UI code, provided its dependencies are similarly independent.",
  "Using .tsx for modules with JSX and .ts for modules without it gives readers a useful clue about the code's role. A model or calculation utility can be found without opening every component file. This is a convention supported by a parsing distinction, not a guarantee that every .tsx file renders UI or every .ts file is independent of React. For example, a custom hook can use React while containing no JSX at all.",
  "Both imports refer to the same Workout module, so the application has one maintained definition of the class. If its validation or derived properties change, both components use that revised definition. Copying a similar class into each component would allow the copies to drift apart. The shared module should own behavior that genuinely belongs to the model, while each component owns its presentation. This reduces duplication without making the model depend on whichever component first needed it.",
  "Importing a runtime value from a component module creates a dependency on that module and potentially its runtime imports. Even if Stats only wants Workout, the organization ties model use to presentation code. Bundlers may remove unused code in production, so the exact emitted bundle depends on the toolchain. A separate model module makes the dependency cleaner regardless: consumers can use Workout without depending on the card module's initialization, JSX setup, or other component-specific concerns.",
  "Suppose WorkoutCard imports WorkoutList, while WorkoutList imports a shared class from WorkoutCard. Those dependencies form a cycle that can make initialization and maintenance harder to reason about. Moving the shared class into Workout.ts lets both components depend on that neutral module instead. The file extension is not what breaks the cycle; the changed dependency direction does. Keep the shared module free of imports back into those components so the new structure does not recreate the same loop.",
  "Fast Refresh tries to preserve component state during development edits when it can update a compatible component boundary. Keeping component exports separate from shared model exports makes that boundary easier to maintain. This is not an absolute rule that every extra export always resets state: integrations can support exceptions such as certain constants, and some component edits reset state anyway. Treat component-only modules as a useful organization practice, not a universal guarantee about every refresh configuration.",
  "The rule checks module exports against the assumptions of the configured Fast Refresh integration. Exporting a model class beside a component can prevent the module from acting as a straightforward component refresh boundary. Moving the model to its own module removes that mixed responsibility. The exact diagnostics and permitted exports depend on configuration; some integrations allow selected constants or framework exports. A warning concerns development refresh behavior, not a claim that JavaScript forbids exporting a class and function together.",
  "A model that depends only on ordinary language features can be constructed and exercised without rendering UI. A unit test can create a Workout and check its derived values directly. The .ts extension is a useful signal, but independence comes from the imports and behavior, not the suffix alone. A .ts file that imports browser globals or React-specific runtime code may still need that environment. Keep those dependencies outside the model when portability is part of the goal.",
  "The test constructs a Workout lasting 75 minutes and checks that isLong is true. It is exercising the model's duration rule directly, so no component or DOM is needed. That makes a failure easier to associate with the business logic rather than rendering setup. Additional boundary cases, such as exactly 60 minutes, would clarify the rule's edge. Component tests can separately check that the interface displays the model's result correctly.",
  "In TSX, angle brackets participate in JSX syntax, so the old <User>data assertion form conflicts with how the parser reads markup. TypeScript therefore requires the as form for assertions in this context. Writing data as User avoids that ambiguity. Both forms, where allowed, are compile-time assertions rather than runtime conversion or validation. Changing the syntax fixes parsing; it does not establish that data actually contains a valid User.",
  "The as syntax is unambiguous in both TS and TSX source, so the assertion can remain the same if code moves between those file types. It tells the checker to view data as User without changing the underlying value. That portability is about source syntax, not runtime safety. If data came from an untrusted response, it still needs validation before the application can confidently rely on User's required fields.",
  "A generic arrow function begins with a type parameter list, which can resemble an opening JSX tag in a .tsx file. The comma in <T,> makes that reading unambiguous, allowing the parser to recognize the generic function. It does not add a second type parameter or change the function's runtime behavior. The function still returns the first array element. Depending on its types and compiler settings, an empty array also needs consideration because there may be no element to return.",
  "A .tsx file can contain code with no JSX, so using that extension broadly is possible when the syntax and project configuration allow it. Doing so loses a helpful distinction between markup-bearing modules and ordinary logic. Some TS syntax also needs adjustment in TSX, notably angle-bracket assertions and ambiguous generic arrow functions. The practical convention is to choose the extension that matches the content rather than assuming TSX is a universally interchangeable spelling for TS.",
  "In a bundler-oriented project such as a typical Vite app, an extensionless import can resolve to a .ts or .tsx source file. Renaming between those extensions may therefore leave the import text unchanged. This is configuration-dependent: native module environments and other resolution modes have different extension rules. Check the project's resolver before treating the convention as universal. Also verify syntax after renaming, since a TSX parser handles some constructs differently from a TS parser.",
  "The jsx compiler option selects how TypeScript handles JSX in emitted output. Some modes transform it into JavaScript calls, while preserve leaves it for a later tool to transform. This setting is separate from the .tsx extension, which identifies source that may contain JSX. Both must fit the project's build pipeline. If one tool preserves markup, another must handle it before the result is delivered to an ordinary JavaScript runtime that cannot parse JSX directly.",
  "This custom hook calls useState and returns an object containing data and an update function. It contains no JSX, so .ts can parse it. That does not make it independent of React: hooks still need to be used under React's rules, normally from a component or another hook. The extension answers a syntax question, while the imported APIs determine runtime requirements. A markup-free file can still participate in React's state system.",
  "Interfaces and type aliases describe values for the checker and commonly live in .ts modules because they need no JSX syntax. A shared types module can let several components agree on the same data contract. Keep those definitions close to the domain they describe rather than collecting unrelated types only for convenience. A type declaration does not create a runtime value, so code that needs a constructor or executable validation must obtain that separately.",
  "import type explicitly imports a name for type checking only. The import is removed from JavaScript output because it supplies no runtime value. This is useful when a module needs to describe Workout values but never constructs one or calls a static method. If code needs new Workout(), it needs a runtime import instead. The distinction makes dependencies clearer and avoids loading a module solely to use an erased type description.",
  "A class defines runtime behavior as well as a type for its instances. After TypeScript removes annotations, JavaScript still needs the constructor and methods so code can create and use instances. An interface, by contrast, only describes a shape and disappears. This explains why a class can be used with new and may be tested with instanceof, while an interface cannot. Runtime class identity and structural compatibility are related but different questions.",
  "A declaration file describes the types and signatures of values implemented elsewhere. It can tell the checker that a library exports a function without supplying the function's executable body. The .d.ts file therefore produces no runtime implementation. If the declared library is missing or behaves differently, the declaration does not repair it. Declaration files are contracts for development tools, so their accuracy matters even though the browser does not execute them.",
  "The code changes minutes on the existing Workout object and then passes that same object back to the state setter. Its identity has not changed, so React may skip the update, and older render snapshots now refer to the mutated object too. Construct a new value representing the changed workout instead. The issue is the mutation of state, not whether the class came from a .ts or .tsx file. File organization does not change React's state update expectations.",
  "Creating a new Workout instance leaves the old instance untouched and gives React a different value by reference. A subsequent render can read the updated minutes from that new object. Preserve every field needed by the model when replacing it; a simplified constructor call may omit data in a more complex class. If the update depends on queued state, use an updater that constructs the replacement from the previous value React supplies.",
  "The class defines a constructor, fields, and a getter, all of which are valid ordinary TypeScript without JSX. Its isLong getter calculates whether minutes exceeds 60; it does not describe an interface element. A .ts file is therefore appropriate. Components can import and use it from their own .tsx modules. The boundary keeps the duration rule testable independently from the markup that displays a workout.",
  "Choose .tsx when a TypeScript source file contains JSX, and .ts when it does not. That rule follows the parser requirement and gives readers a useful organizational clue. Do not decide solely from whether the module is part of a React project: models, helpers, and hooks without JSX can use .ts. The extension does not establish runtime independence or guarantee hot-reload behavior. Those depend on the module's actual code, imports, and build configuration."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: frontend-development/typescript-ts-vs-tsx");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
